.TH READLINK 1 2026-10-31 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
readlink \- print the target of a symbolic link, or a canonical path
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B readlink
.RB [ \-fnqsv ]
.I file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B readlink
prints the target of the symbolic link
.IR file ,
followed by a newline.
With
.BR \-f ,
it prints the canonical absolute path of
.I file
instead.
It takes exactly one operand, and any other number is a usage error.
This
.B readlink
is the
.BR busybox (1)
applet, which has fewer options than GNU
.BR readlink ;
see
.BR NOTES .
.SS Links
A link is a symbolic link, a directory junction such as
.BR jn (1)
makes, or an app execution alias, one of the program names that Windows
keeps in a user's
.I WindowsApps
directory.
The target of an alias is the path of the program it runs.
A hard link is an ordinary file.
.PP
Without
.BR \-f ,
.B readlink
reads the link and prints its target as stored, without resolving it or
checking that it exists.
A relative target stays relative, and Windows resolves it from the
directory that holds the link.
Backslashes become slashes.
The NT prefix that Windows stores with an absolute target, such as
.BR \e??\e ,
is removed, and a network target is printed in the form
.IR //server/share/dir .
A folder where a volume is mounted is read too, and its target is
printed exactly as stored, as in
\fB\e??\eVolume{\fIguid\fB}\e\fR.
.PP
Anything else is an error without
.BR \-f :
an ordinary file or directory, a missing file, or another kind of
reparse point.
A
.B .lnk
shortcut is an ordinary file.
.SS Canonical paths
With
.BR \-f ,
.I file
need not be a link.
The result is an absolute path with forward slashes.
Dot and dot-dot components, repeated separators, and a trailing
separator are removed.
A dot-dot is removed by name, before any link is followed, so
.IB link /..\&
is the directory that holds
.IR link ,
and the component that a dot-dot removes need not exist.
Every link left in the path is followed.
Each name has the case and the long form that it has on disk, so
.B c:/progra~1
becomes
.BR "C:/Program Files" .
.PP
The file must exist, with one exception.
If only its last component is missing, the rest of the path is
canonicalized, and the component is appended as typed after a slash.
The rest must exist, but need not be a directory.
A missing name with no directory part, such as
.BR new.o ,
is in the current directory, as if it were
.BR ./new.o .
A missing name directly in a root directory, such as
.B c:/new.o
or
.BR /new.o ,
is in that root, canonicalized like any other directory, so the drive
must exist.
.PP
If
.I file
is a link whose target is missing, the target is canonicalized by the
same rules.
A relative target is taken relative to the directory that holds the
link.
.SS Output
The path ends with a line feed, never a carriage return and line feed.
When
.I file
cannot be read, nothing is written to standard output.
With
.BR \-v ,
a failure without
.B \-f
is reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
readlink: \fIfile\fR: cannot read link: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
where
.I reason
is a system message.
It is
.B No such file or directory
for a missing file, and
.B not a symlink
for a file or directory that is not a link.
A failure with
.B \-f
is reported as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
readlink: \fIfile\fR: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-fn ,
and may follow the operand, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
There are no long options.
.TP
.B \-n
Do not write the newline after the path.
.TP
.B \-f
Print the canonical absolute path of
.I file
instead of the target of a link; see
.BR "Canonical paths" .
.TP
.B \-v
Report a failure on standard error; see
.BR Output .
Failures are silent otherwise, and the exit status is the only sign.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-s
Accepted and ignored.
Failures are silent unless
.B \-v
is given, and these do not change that.
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument only, write a usage message on standard output and
exit with status 0.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS
When it is
.BR 1 ,
Windows shows its critical-error dialogs, such as for a drive with no
disk in it, instead of the operation failing with an error.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the arguments are expanded when busybox starts, for a caller
such as
.B cmd.exe
that does not expand them.
When it is unset, busybox sets it to
.B 0
for the programs it starts, so commands run from the kit's shell get the
shell's expansion only.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, the first operand ends the options.
.PP
Output written to a console also depends on
.BR BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION ,
.BR BB_TERMINAL_MODE ,
.BR CONEMUPID ,
.BR LANG ,
.BR LC_ALL ,
and
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
as for every busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The path was written.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid command line, with a usage message on standard error, a
failure to read the link or to canonicalize the path, or an error
writing standard output.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show where a junction points:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ readlink vendor/zlib
C:/src/zlib\-1.3.1
.EE
.RE
.PP
Test whether a path is a link, by exit status:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ readlink vendor/zlib >/dev/null && echo link
link
.EE
.RE
.PP
Say why a file is not a link:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ readlink \-v main.c
readlink: main.c: cannot read link: not a symlink
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the canonical path of a directory, and of a file that does not
exist yet in a directory that does:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cd C:/src/app
$ readlink \-f ../lib/./include/..
C:/src/lib
$ readlink \-f build/new.o
C:/src/app/build/new.o
.EE
.RE
.PP
A bare name that does not exist is in the current directory:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ readlink \-f new.o
C:/src/app/new.o
.EE
.RE
.PP
Expand a short name to the real one:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ readlink \-f c:/progra~1
C:/Program Files
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR readlink :
.IP \(bu 2
Only
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-n ,
and
.B \-v
do anything.
.BR \-q " and " \-s
are ignored, and
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-m ,
.BR \-z ,
and the long options are invalid.
.IP \(bu 2
Exactly one operand is allowed.
.IP \(bu 2
A file that is not a link is reported as
.B not a symlink
rather than
.BR "Invalid argument" .
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-f
removes a dot-dot before following the link that precedes it, where GNU
.B readlink
follows the link first.
.PP
.B readlink \-f
prints what
.BR realpath (1)
prints for the same operand.
.PP
Symbolic links need Developer Mode or an elevated administrator to
create; see
.BR ln (1).
A junction needs neither; see
.BR jn (1).
.B readlink
reads both.
.PP
Paths may have drive letters and either kind of slash.
Names may contain any Unicode character on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
.PP
Paths are limited to 259 characters.
Without
.BR \-f ,
a longer path fails with
.BR "No such file or directory" .
.B \-f
treats a path of 260 or more characters, as typed or once made absolute,
as if its last component were missing: the component is appended as
typed without being checked, and if the rest is too long as well,
.B \-f
fails.
When the canonical path of an existing file would have 260 or more
characters,
.B \-f
fails.
.PP
From
.BR cmd.exe ,
.B readlink
expands wildcards in its operand itself; see
.BR BB_GLOBBING .
A pattern that matches several names gives several operands, which is a
usage error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR jn (1),
.BR ln (1),
.BR ls (1),
.BR realpath (1)
